xnu 0421: kern.sweet_pace: leeway, idle periods and the A53 cluster for the kernel's polling threads, and their names

The port takes no device interrupt but the GPU's (0419): modem_watch (GLINK/QMI, WLAN copy engines and HTT, IPA),
adsp_thread (GLINK to the ADSP, audio, FastRPC's polls), fr_thread, the USB controller's ud_dp_thread, the touch
panel, the keys and en0's net_thread look at their hardware from kernel threads of priority 81 between sleeps of
1-20 ms. Report 16 (§3.2, §5.3) measures ~3600-4100 wakeups a second of such threads on the A73s, four of them every
~1.2 ms (IOSleep(1)): a possible preemption of backboardd's render thread each (61-112 a second measured) and a
migration between A73s (66-105 a second), and heat (17 §4.4: iOS idles ~20 C above Android).

New file bsd/dev/sweet_pace.c. kern.sweet_pace (boot-arg sweet_pace), 0 by default: every loop sleeps as before
(modem_watch and adsp_thread 1 ms, fr_thread 2 ms, USB 1 ms idle and 125 us busy, touch 10 ms up / 4 ms or 0.5 ms
down, keys 20 ms, net 2 or 10 ms), with no leeway and no binding. With 1:
  - each idle sleep carries kern.sweet_pace_leeway_pct (25) % of its period as leeway, at most 2 ms
    (assert_wait_deadline_with_leeway, TIMEOUT_URGENCY_LEEWAY), so the timers can coalesce; the busy ones (USB with
    traffic, touch with a finger down) keep none;
  - modem_watch and adsp_thread sleep kern.sweet_pace_idle_ms (4; 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10 or 20, divisors of 1000) once
    their activity count has not moved for kern.sweet_pace_hold_ms (200), and 1 ms again as soon as it moves.
    Activity: modem, g_rx_msgs + g_tx_msgs + htt_msgs + htt_msdus + etxq_tail + ipa_tx_sent + the IPA LAN and MRX
    frames; ADSP, a_rx_msgs + a_tx_msgs + a_writes_done + a_gpr_rx_count. Their millisecond counters (ms, a_ms)
    count loops as before and add the long sleep's milliseconds, landing on multiples of the period so that
    "ms % 1000 == 0" (ipa_poll's GSI error check) still comes; below 60000 (the boot's timed steps: logs at 3, 5,
    20, 30 and 44 s, the audio test at 45 s) they keep 1 ms. topaz_wlan_transmit() and topaz_ipa_tx() wake
    modem_watch at once (sweet_pace_kick) instead of leaving the frame for its next look;
  - ud_dp_thread sleeps kern.sweet_pace_usb_idle_us (4000, 1000-20000) instead of 1 ms when no frame moved in the
    last 10 ms; ud_dp_output still wakes it at once, and a frame from the host waits that long at most;
  - the threads in kern.sweet_pace_ecore (a bit each; all by default) are soft-bound to the A53 cluster
    (thread_bind_cluster_type(current_thread(), 'E', soft)), where the render thread never runs. Each thread
    applies a change to kern.sweet_pace or kern.sweet_pace_ecore itself, before its next sleep (no lock held there).
Whatever the mode, the threads are named (thread_set_thread_name): bit 0 "sweet modem", 1 "sweet adsp", 2 "sweet
usb", 3 "sweet touch", 4 "sweet net", 5 "sweet keys", 6 "sweet frpc"; report 16 §5.3 could only tell them by their
period. kern.sweet_pace_stats: per thread, sleeps, idle sleeps, wakeups by a kick, A53 or any.

Not changed: touch with a finger down (0.5 ms grid of 0313; the IC's line is TLMM GPIO 80, whose summary interrupt
is SPI 227, not routed), the USB busy loop, the vsync timer (120 Hz, software; the MDP's vsync is in MDSS SPI 186),
UFS command waits, the 100 ms USB log, the 1 s UFS log.

Expected [not measured]: with kern.sweet_pace=1 and an idle system, wakeups of modem_watch and adsp_thread from ~830
to ~210 a second each, USB from ~830 to ~210, and none of them on an A73 while the A53s have room. Risks: up to the
idle period of latency on the first frame received by WLAN, IPA or USB and on the first audio buffer after a quiet
spell; WLAN, audio and USB work on the A53s. Protocol: dev-notes/investigacion/anexos/16-despertares/02-parches.md.
